  "id": "DrugBank_DB00624 - MONDO_18068",
  "verbalization": "Testosterone is implicated in the pathogenesis of Trisomy 13 through a chain of indications that link it to Cryptorchidism, a condition associated with testosterone therapy. The evidence suggests that Testosterone, when administered as Testosterone Cypionate or Testosterone Enanthate, may contribute to the development of Trisomy 13, possibly through indirect mechanisms involving hormonal imbalances or developmental effects. The presence of Fluoxymesterone in the pathway, a synthetic testosterone analog, further supports this link, as it also exhibits indications pointing to Trisomy 13. The integration of these pathways highlights a potential role of testosterone-based therapies in the etiology of Trisomy 13, though the exact mechanism remains unclear. The evidence is consistent across multiple pathways, suggesting a possible dependency on testosterone in the development of this condition, though further research is needed to elucidate the precise biological pathways involved.",
